From: Grizbeard <justin.morken@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 21:10:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] Let an effect unit be a wet-only send/return

[AI-generated text: this description was written autonomously by an AI
agent (Claude). Review it before relying on it or submitting it.]

A standard effect unit is an insert: it crossfades its effects' output
against the signal it was given. That cannot make an effects return. At
Dry/Wet 1.0 the dry signal still gets through Echo and Reverb, because
both output only their tail and EngineEffectChain adds their input back
before the crossfade; Dry+Wet always adds it; and a unit that is off, or
has every slot off or empty, passes its input straight through. Routed to
a bus that feeds its own output, any of those puts a second copy of the
mix on it.

[EffectRack1_EffectUnitN],wet_only makes the unit a send/return instead:

  output = effects(input * mix)

The mix knob becomes the send level into the effects, nothing of the
input reaches the output except through them, and the output is silence
whenever nothing processed. Closing the send stops feeding an echo
without cutting its tail, so a knob that follows a hardware channel fader
is a post-fader send. Starting and stopping fade over one callback so
that neither steps. A channel the unit is not routed to is left alone,
as in the other modes.

wet_only is a property of the unit, not of the chain preset loaded into
it: it is not saved in or restored from presets, since a preset that put
a return back into Dry/Wet would put the dry mix on its output. It is
persisted in the config, and sent to the engine once it exists - the
EffectChain constructor sends the chain's parameters before a subclass
can add to them.

Crossfader buses carried no track features, so a tempo-synced effect on
one - Echo, above all - fell back to a delay in seconds. A bus holding
exactly one channel now takes that channel's features, as the headphone
mix already does for a single PFL channel.

engineeffectchain_wetonly_test drives an EngineEffectChain with the
built-in Echo, and covers the Dry/Wet leak itself, no dry output, the
echo arriving, the tail surviving a closed send, a closed send feeding
nothing, silence when switched off or empty, and an unrouted channel
left untouched.

[End of AI-generated text.]

